# The wheels here ARE committed, deliberately. Nothing is ignored; this file
# exists only to say why, because an earlier version of it ignored them and the
# reasoning needs to be visible where the decision is.
#
# Building an emscripten wheel needs ~1 GB of toolchain -- Pyodide's patched
# emsdk, a Rust nightly and a wasm-exception-handling sysroot -- which the docs
# workflow has no reason to carry on every run. So `build-wheel.sh` is run by
# hand and its output is committed, and the deploy only copies files. This is
# what POUNCE does with `pounce-solver`, and the two must agree: the page
# installs both wheels into one interpreter.
#
# The cost is that a wheel can go stale. An emscripten wheel is valid for
# exactly one Pyodide build, so if either version moves and nobody rebuilds,
# the page breaks. Two things catch that instead of a visitor:
#
#   * `build-wheel.sh --check` runs in CI, and compares the staged manifests'
#     `pyodide_version` against the pin in `worker.js` and the wheel version
#     against `pyproject.toml`.
#   * `worker.js` re-checks the same thing at load and refuses with a message
#     naming the rebuild command.
#
# Neither catches a wheel that is merely *old* -- built from a commit whose
# Python source has since changed. Rebuild and re-commit when the page should
# show new solver behaviour; the version check will not remind you.
